course: myths
lesson: 17
title: Magnets, compasses and big Earth facts
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: "Thunder? That's just two big clouds bumping their heads, love," says Grandad, the way his own grandad told him. It's a lovely picture, and so are the others: the compass needle pointing straight at Father Christmas's house, the Earth's middle sloshing with lava like a giant soup pot, Greenland on the classroom map as big as all of Africa.
explain: But the real Earth is stranger than the stories. A compass points to the magnetic north pole, which sits in a different place from the North Pole and wanders a little every year; and in the middle of our planet there's a solid ball of iron and nickel about as hot as the surface of the Sun.
explain: Winter isn't cold because the Sun is far away: the Earth is actually closest to the Sun in early January! Winter is cold because the Earth is tilted, so the light comes in low and slanted, spread thinly, and the days are short.
explain: Flat maps fool us too: you can't peel a ball flat without stretching it, so common maps stretch places near the poles, and Greenland looks enormous when Africa is really about 14 times bigger.
explain: When a friend clings to a story, a surprising fact or a quick look at a globe works better than "you're wrong": "Did you know we're nearer the Sun in January?"
fact: A compass points to the magnetic north pole, which is in a different place from the geographic North Pole and slowly moves every year.
fact: Winter is colder because the Earth's tilt makes sunlight come in low and spread out and the days short; the Earth is nearest the Sun in early January.
fact: Volcanoes are found in cold places too, such as Iceland, Alaska and Antarctica, and Arthur's Seat in Edinburgh is an old volcano.
fact: The Earth's inner core is a solid ball of iron and nickel about as hot as the Sun's surface; the outer core is liquid metal.
fact: Lava comes from melted rock in the mantle (the deep layer of hot rock under the crust, the Earth's outer skin) and crust, not from the core.
fact: Africa is a continent of 54 countries with thousands of languages.
fact: Common flat world maps stretch places near the poles, so Greenland looks huge; in fact Africa is about 14 times bigger than Greenland.
fact: The Sun has about 5 billion years of fuel left, so it will not burn out for an extremely long time.
fact: Clouds are made of tiny drops of liquid water or tiny ice crystals; real steam is an invisible gas, and the white "steam" above a kettle is also tiny drops.
fact: Thunder is the bang made when lightning heats the air extremely fast and the air expands suddenly; clouds do not bump together.
fact: A rainbow is a smooth spread of countless colours; Isaac Newton chose to name seven, partly to match the seven notes of a musical scale.
exercise: Ollie at Scouts: "A compass points to the North Pole." What's more exact? | It points to the magnetic north pole, which is somewhere else | It points exactly to the North Pole every time | It points to the North Star in the sky | It points to the nearest big magnet hidden in the ground below
exercise: What does the magnetic north pole do over the years? | It slowly moves | It stays fixed at the North Pole | It swaps with the South Pole every year | It moves round the equator
exercise: Leah's homework group: "It's colder in winter because the Sun is further away." What's the real reason? | The tilt makes winter sunlight low and spread out, and days short | The Sun is much further away in winter | The Sun gives out less heat in winter | Clouds block the Sun all winter long
exercise: When is the Earth closest to the Sun? | In early January | In late June | In the middle of August | It is always the same distance
exercise: Henry: "Volcanoes are only in hot countries." Which shows he's wrong? | Iceland is full of volcanoes | Volcanoes make every country hot | Italy has a volcano | Volcanoes are only on islands
exercise: Where in a cold city can you climb an old volcano? | Arthur's Seat in Edinburgh | Big Ben in London | The Eiffel Tower in Paris | Blackpool Tower by the sea
exercise: Kayla: "The Earth's core is just a big lava lake." What is the inner core really? | A solid ball of iron and nickel | A great big lake of hot runny lava | A hollow space full of hot gases | A ball of strange hot ice under pressure
exercise: Where does the lava from volcanoes come from? | Melted rock in the mantle and crust | A pipe going straight down to the core | Melted ice from deep under the ground | Rain that has been heated by the Sun
exercise: Reggie, not meaning harm: "Africa is a country." What's true? | Africa is a continent of 54 countries | Africa is one country with 54 cities | Africa is a country with one language | Africa is an island near Europe
exercise: Jess points at the classroom map: "Greenland is as big as Africa!" What's true? | Africa is about 14 times bigger; flat maps stretch the top | They really are the same size | Greenland is twice as big as Africa | Greenland shrinks every winter, when lots of its ice melts away
exercise: Ryan's little sister is scared: "The Sun will burn out soon!" What can you tell her? | It has about 5 billion years of fuel left | It will burn out in about 100 years from now | It is already starting to go out | It needs new fuel from Earth every year
exercise: Lily: "Clouds are made of steam, like a kettle." What are clouds made of? | Tiny drops of water or ice crystals | Invisible steam gas, as from a kettle | Smoke drifting up from factories and fires | Cotton-like dust blown up from the ground
exercise: What is real steam like? | It is an invisible gas | It is white and fluffy | It is made of tiny ice crystals | It is the same as smoke
exercise: Grandad: "Thunder is clouds bumping together." What makes thunder? | Lightning heats air so fast it expands with a bang | Two clouds crashing into each other high in the sky | Rain hitting the ground very hard | Wind whistling through the clouds
exercise: Owen: "Rainbows have seven colours, everybody knows that." What's true? | A rainbow is a smooth blend; seven is a choice Newton made | There are exactly seven, no more, no less | There are only three real colours in it | Each rainbow has a different number of stripes, from three to ten
exercise: Why did Newton pick seven colours? | Partly to match the seven musical notes | Because he counted seven stripes exactly | Because there are seven days in a week only | Because the King ordered it
test: You're on a hike far in the north of Canada and the compass needle points a bit off from the real North Pole. What is going on? | It points to magnetic north, which is in a different place | The compass is broken and needs a new battery to work again | The North Pole moved there last night | Compasses stop working in the cold
test: "bruh it's cold cos we're further from the sun, obviously" What do you say? | Actually we're nearest the Sun in January; it's the slant of the light | Yes, and in summer we touch the Sun | Yes, the Earth goes far away every winter | No, it's cold because the Moon blocks the Sun
test: What would happen to winter if the Earth had no tilt at all? | There would be almost no seasons, and days would stay about the same | Winter would last all year everywhere | Every country would get summer at once twice a year | The Earth would stop going round the Sun
test: A cartoon shows a hero digging to the middle of the Earth and splashing into a lava lake. What would he really find? | A liquid metal outer core, then a solid iron and nickel ball | A big lava lake, just like the cartoon | Cold rock all the way through | A hollow cave with a little sun inside
test: Your mate goes, "Shut up, I'm literally looking at the map, Greenland's massive." Best move? | Look at a globe together; flat maps stretch the top | Rip the map off the wall | Tell him he's stupid | Agree with him, maps are always exactly right about sizes
test: Someone at the park says, "I'm going on holiday to Africa, they speak African there." What's a kind and true reply? | Which country? Africa has 54, with thousands of languages | Yes, everybody there speaks African | No, they all speak English there | Laugh at them in front of everyone
test: Your kettle is boiling. Just above the spout there's a small clear gap, then a white cloud. What is the white cloud? | Tiny water drops formed as the steam cools | Pure invisible steam | Smoke coming off the hot metal element inside | Air turned white by the heat
test: You see lightning, then hear thunder a few seconds later. What made the bang? | The air heated by the lightning expanding suddenly | The clouds bumping together after the flash | The lightning hitting the ground and echoing back up | Raindrops crashing together in the cloud
test: Grandad tells his clouds-bumping story with a big smile. How do you argue wisely? | Say it's a lovely picture, then share the real reason kindly | Tell him he's too old to know science | Refuse to listen to his stories any more | Pretend to agree with him, and then laugh at him later with friends
test: You shine sunlight through a glass of water and look closely at the rainbow patch. What do you see? | Colours blending smoothly into each other | Exactly seven stripes with dark gaps between | Only red and blue, with nothing in between | A plain white light with no colours at all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the cause of lower temperatures during the winter months? | The inclination of the Earth's axis causes oblique sunlight and abbreviated days | The Earth is then at its greatest distance from the Sun | The Sun's output of heat diminishes during winter | The Moon intercepts a portion of the sunlight
exam: Identify the location toward which the needle of a magnetic compass is directed. | The magnetic north pole, which is distinct from the geographic pole | The geographic North Pole precisely | The star known as Polaris | The nearest deposit of iron ore
exam: It is given that common flat maps of the world represent Greenland as comparable in area to Africa. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Africa exceeds Greenland in area approximately fourteenfold | The two land masses are of equal area | Greenland exceeds Africa in area | The areas of the two land masses cannot be determined at present
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the cause of thunder. | It results from the sudden expansion of air heated by lightning | It results from the collision of clouds | It results from the impact of heavy rain upon the surface of the ground | It results from wind passing over mountains
